f 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147705 #edb/ref 1045 "Forced Oscillators" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147705 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186148288 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147705 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048622 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147705 #edb/ref 1050 960.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147705 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "ddf081b5-4271-58f1-ac92-6ac95f2e57d1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148288 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186148307 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148288 #edb/ref 1061 24279 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148288 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e32b8ecd-fee2-56e7-867d-2ac1f9f9b872" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148288 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186148747 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148307 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186148317 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148307 #edb/ref 1061 24721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148307 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "39e8e7e0-57a8-511a-aef2-6b002039cb19" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148307 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186147448 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148308 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186148318 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148308 #edb/ref 1061 24722 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148308 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0dd1a25e-e81d-5bf2-9e52-1d14c32c9689" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148308 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186147449 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148309 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186148310 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148309 #edb/ref 1061 24723 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148309 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f42f709a-7c4f-5416-bf43-29a32c645bd5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148309 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186147450 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148310 #edb/ref 1061 24724 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148310 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "dbf9225d-b92c-5540-96eb-190970598cab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148310 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186147451 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148317 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186148308 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148317 #edb/ref 1061 25340 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148317 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "62673a11-379c-5680-872b-45a2328762ae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148317 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186148766 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148318 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186148309 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148318 #edb/ref 1061 25341 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148318 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4be5106f-eac3-59d0-8ca0-5316fe821456" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148318 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186148767 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148747 #edb/ref 1064 "A harmonic oscillator is said to be **forced** if it is subject to a nonzero external force $f(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\ny'' + by' + \\omega^2 y = f(t)\n$$\n\nIf the external force $f(t)$ is periodic and its frequency is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution, then the system experiences **resonance** and the amplitude of the oscillations increases without bound over time.\n\nFor example, consider the following differential equation which represents a harmonic oscillator subject to a forcing function $\\cos(2t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\ny''+4y=\\cos(2t), \\qquad y(0) =0, \\qquad y'(0) = 0\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution $y_c$ here represents simple harmonic motion,\n\n$$\ny_c=A\\cos(2 t) + B\\sin(2 t).\n$$\n\nThe forcing function $f(t) = \\cos(2t)$ has the same frequency as the complementary solution above (both have a frequency of $\\Omega = 2 \\, \\textrm{s}^{-1}$), so the system experiences *resonance*. As a result,  the particular solution\n\n$$\ny_p = \\dfrac{t}{4}\\sin(2t)\n$$\n\nhas an amplitude that is proportional to $t,$ which means that the amplitude will increase over time.\n\nThe general solution in this case is\n\n$$\ny(t) = A\\cos(2 t) + B\\sin(2 t) +\\dfrac{t}{4}\\sin(2t),\n$$\n\nand taking into account the initial conditions, we get\n\n$$\ny(t)=\\dfrac{t}{4}\\sin(2t).\n$$\n\nNote that the amplitude of his function grows linearly. The graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/90/9032f93f083a100b14f732ba114e306fd0c9c5e0576ce218c9248c0e140854de.png)\n\n**Important:** Not all forced oscillators experience resonance. In the above example, if we change the forcing function to $f(t) = \\cos(t),$ resulting in the equation\n\n$$\ny''+4y=\\cos(t), \\qquad y(0) =0, \\qquad y'(0) = 0,\n$$\n\n then the amplitude of the particular solution is constant:\n\n$$\ny_p = \\dfrac{1}{3}\\cos(t)\n$$\n\nThere is no resonance in this case since the frequency of the external periodic force $f(t)=\\cos(t)$ does not coincide with the frequency of the complementary solution, which is $\\Omega = 2.$\n\nThe general solution in this case is\n\n$$\ny(t) = A\\cos(2 t) + B\\sin(2 t) +\\dfrac{1}{3}\\cos(t),\n$$\n\nand taking into account the initial conditions, we get\n\n$$\ny(t)=\\dfrac{1}{3}\\left(\\cos(t)-\\cos(2t)\\right).\n$$\n\nAs we can see in the graph below, the amplitude of the solution does not grow.\n\n![](images/0e/0eac5b659d3ea9712a3672eb443227d7c8cee5377ff52d7646ca263dc5e11007.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148747 #edb/ref 1065 4069 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148747 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "77b16ea3-5ad1-5108-9c77-d314e6e9ddf3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148747 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148766 #edb/ref 1064 "Remember that, if an object of mass $m$ is attached to a spring with spring constant $k,$ and the damping coefficient is $c,$ then the equation of motion is\n\n$$\nmy'' = -cy'-ky.\n$$\n\nIf there is an external force $F(t),$ then the equation of motion is\n\n$$\nm y'' =-cy' -k y + F(t) .\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148766 #edb/ref 1065 4169 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148766 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ab390ee6-c457-55d4-a265-9d62a1955ab8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148766 #edb/ref 1067 "The Equation of Motion for a Forced Spring-Mass System" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148767 #edb/ref 1064 "Given a forced harmonic motion equation *without* damping,\n\n$$\ny'' + \\omega^2 y = f(t),\n$$\n\nthe complementary solution can be written in three different forms:\n\n1\\. $y_c = R\\cos{(\\omega t + \\phi_1)}$\n\n2\\. $y_c = R\\sin{(\\omega t + \\phi_2)}$\n\n3\\. $y_c =A\\cos{\\omega t} + B\\sin{\\omega t}$\n\nAlternatively, given a forced harmonic motion equation *with* damping,\n\n$$\ny'' + by' + \\omega^2 y = f(t),\n$$\n\nif the roots of the characteristic equation are $\\lambda = p \\pm \\textrm{i}q,$ then the complementary solution can be written in three different forms:\n\n1\\. $y_c = R e^{p t} \\cos{(q t + \\phi_1)}$\n\n2\\. $y_c = R e^{p t} \\sin{(q t + \\phi_2)}$\n\n3\\. $y_c =e^{p t} \\left( A\\cos{q t} + B\\sin{q t} \\right)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148767 #edb/ref 1065 4170 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148767 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "b4683094-19f8-557e-b0c7-7aad98cab72c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148767 #edb/ref 1067 "General Solutions to Forced Harmonic Motion Equations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333219 #edb/ref 1080 "{y}^{″}+4.5y=3{t}^{3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333219 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333219 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1944db5d-5dbb-5268-81cc-0ce9a0503edf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333710 #edb/ref 1080 "{y}^{″}+4.5y=12{t}^{3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333710 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333710 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4ea66d73-08a4-526b-8c6d-aaa97b9b7f91" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186334358 #edb/ref 1080 "{y}^{″}+4.5y=6{t}^{3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186334358 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186334358 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "9aa2a08d-f61a-5f63-8878-f2e9f1fa96ba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186334419 #edb/ref 1080 "{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+2y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186334419 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186334419 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a28c4186-c812-5117-83c3-943c9871c988" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186334798 #edb/ref 1080 "{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+2y=15\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186334798 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186334798 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "cf4057bb-935e-5406-ae84-0e99a9b2feff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186334805 #edb/ref 1080 "{y}^{″}+9y=3{t}^{3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186334805 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186334805 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d02a9da6-04d1-5120-b8a8-87bf90b8110c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186334819 #edb/ref 1080 "{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+3y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186334819 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186334819 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d13828c4-d92d-5786-a8a2-c5df12308498" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186334880 #edb/ref 1080 "{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+3y=15\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186334880 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186334880 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d7d2a3b1-1549-5cd5-ad78-61414e7f333c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335109 #edb/ref 1080 "{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+4y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335109 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335109 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f1aff7fe-125a-5328-8e93-9bb5ada34908" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335226 #edb/ref 1080 "{y}^{″}+9y=12{t}^{3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335226 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335226 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "fe0e4ecf-325b-59bd-90a8-d08faeba5d61" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335318 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186333219 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335318 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186333710 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335318 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186334358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335318 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186334805 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335318 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186335226 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335318 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335318 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335318 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186333219 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335318 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "2b1b9c5a-ea8e-581e-b135-39bd76d3bc2c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335359 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186334419 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335359 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186334798 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335359 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186334819 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335359 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186334880 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335359 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186335109 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335359 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335359 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335359 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186335109 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335359 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "3cbe8b04-b9bb-5077-ad66-1fa7004e8e05" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335722 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335318 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335722 #edb/ref 1069 "q-64599" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335722 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335722 #edb/ref 1071 "A particle of mass $4\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=18\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$ \nAn external force $F(t)=12{t}^{3}\\,\\text{N}$ is applied to the particle.\n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion without damping, what is the governing equation?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335722 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "07b06f33-b4ee-5f40-b29c-4546e0a946ba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335722 #edb/ref 1074 "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with forced harmonic motion without damping under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ and  external force $F(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m,$ $k$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n4{y}^{″}=-18y+12{t}^{3}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4.5y=3{t}^{3}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335723 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335359 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335723 #edb/ref 1069 "q-64600" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335723 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335723 #edb/ref 1071 "A particle of mass $3\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=12\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$  The particle is also subject to the resistive force that's proportional to the particle's velocity with a damping coefficient $c=9\\,\\text{kg/s},$ and an external force given by $F(t)=15\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,\\text{N}.$   \n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds.  If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion, what is the governing equation?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335723 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "23f2ae10-1325-5e77-a3d0-434c6abe321a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335723 #edb/ref 1074 "The general equation of forced harmonic motion with damping is\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-c{y}^{′}-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m,$ $k,$ $c$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n3{y}^{″}=-9{y}^{′}-12y+15\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+4y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354795 #edb/ref 1080 "I only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354795 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354795 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0e3922d0-9e98-50fc-badf-c5493ce5c08a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355576 #edb/ref 1080 "II and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355576 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355576 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "66a11be1-b8f6-5bf3-865a-32797a40b01d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355803 #edb/ref 1080 "I and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355803 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355803 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "81aec1db-2243-5668-a3b3-f7f2049fd7a2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356074 #edb/ref 1080 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356074 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356074 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "9e22c7d3-b846-5397-9fb1-05bb1733229d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356287 #edb/ref 1080 "III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356287 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356287 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b95128ac-6579-590f-8e02-4fa489a727c1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356917 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186354795 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356917 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186355576 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356917 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186355803 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356917 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186356074 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356917 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186356287 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356917 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356917 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356917 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186356287 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356917 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "03b4326e-a9f7-5be8-a7ee-fabfc0ce13c4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357739 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186356917 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357739 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94382" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357739 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357739 #edb/ref 1071 "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+25y=24\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\nI. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(25t+ϕ)+12\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$\n\nII. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t+ϕ)+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$\n\nIII. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R$ and $ϕ$ are constants." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357739 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cbe86c7f-4360-5aea-8ad0-c457c70df4e7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357739 #edb/ref 1074 "The general solution to this equation is the sum of the complementary function and a particular solution.\n\nFirst, we find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(t),$ which is the solution to the simple harmonic motion equation ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.$ It can be written in three different forms:\n\n1\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)$\n\n2\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)$\n\n3\\. ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\nIn our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=25\\,⟹\\,\\omega =5.\n$$\n\nThus, we have:\n\n1\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t+ϕ)$\n\n2\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t+ϕ)$\n\n3\\. ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t$\n\nNext, we find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t).$ The particular solution is of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution into the differential equation and equating coefficients, we find that $\\alpha =1$ and $\\beta =0.$ So, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n1\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t+ϕ)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$\n\n2\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t+ϕ)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$\n\n3\\. ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$\n\nOnly III matches the forms above. Therefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186428721 #edb/ref 1030 0.5 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428721 #edb/ref 1032 "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account differential: 1 rises / 2 eligible passed source activities = 0.500000; nearest selected fraction = 1/2. Crossing activity numbers [1]; waits [1]. Separate account estimates: differential=1/2 (1/2). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: True. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/differential-frequency-estimates.json, pair 2526->2549. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428721 #edb/ref 1036 #edb/ref 17592186050032 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186445728 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186445743 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445728 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186445744 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445728 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186445745 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445728 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186445746 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445728 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186445747 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445728 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445728 #edb/ref 1045 "Forced Oscillators" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445728 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186445747 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445728 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 1